The letters are joined after _trim_long_words, which trims per character with the Japanese thresholds, so a sung letter stretched over a pause is still shortened before it becomes part of a word. Word ends come from Whisper's spaces (via _mark_spaces), punctuation other than an apostrophe (it's), and a small letter followed by a capital, because for Japanese Whisper joins its pieces with no space (sadSometimes); that heuristic would split a name like McDonald, which seemed rarer than song lines. Latin words bypass the Japanese/Chinese tokenizer as phrases of their own instead of being fed to fugashi, which treats a run of Latin letters as one unknown token and gave arbitrary splits. A space is only added between two Latin words, not after Japanese text (20分後。OK。), matching Japanese typography and the existing Easy Japaneseは test.

Correction: fugashi doesn't make one unknown token of joined Latin text; it splits it into mostly single letters plus one arbitrary lump ('S','o','m',…,'imesIfeelsohappyButmostly'), which is where the splits between letters came from.
